  "id": "DrugBank_DB09074 - MONDO_16093",
  "verbalization": "Olaparib, a poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitor, is shown to have a clinical relationship with Borderline Epithelial Tumor Of Ovary through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Olaparib may exert its effects on this tumor type through a shared mechanism with Altretamine, a chemotherapy agent that is also indicated for Borderline Epithelial Tumor Of Ovary. This connection is supported by the presence of a common pathway involving Altretamine, which is linked to the same tumor type. The integration of these pathways implies that Olaparib may act through a mechanism that is relevant to the biological processes underlying Borderline Epithelial Tumor Of Ovary, potentially through the inhibition of PARP enzymes, which are involved in DNA repair and could lead to cell death in tumor cells. The presence of multiple pathways connecting Olaparib to this tumor type, along with the shared indication with Altretamine, supports the hypothesis that Olaparib may have a role in the treatment of this tumor.",
